          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What counts as insomnia rather than just a few bad nights?",
          "answer": "Doctors generally call a sleep problem **chronic insomnia** when it meets all three of these conditions:\n\n- **Frequency:** trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early happens at least three nights a week.\n- **Duration:** it lasts at least three months.\n- **Daytime impact:** it causes daytime effects such as fatigue, poor concentration, low mood, or reduced performance.\n\nThe daytime part matters. Short sleep without daytime problems is not treated as a disorder. (Source: *Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*.)\n\nA few bad nights, or a short stretch of poor sleep after a stressful event, does not meet these criteria. Assessment usually includes a sleep diary kept for one to two weeks. Clinicians also check for other conditions that can look like insomnia, such as sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, depression, anxiety, pain, and effects from caffeine, alcohol, or medicines. (Source: same guideline.)\n\nFor coding, insomnia appears in two places in the US version of ICD-10. Insomnia from an organic or medical cause sits under G47 in the nervous system chapter. Insomnia not due to a substance or a known physical condition sits under F51 in the mental and behavioral chapter. (Source: *Coding insomnia: G47 and F51*.)\n\nSome signs suggest a different cause that needs assessment, not just sleep habit changes. These include loud snoring with pauses in breathing, heavy daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, acting out dreams, or poor sleep alongside low mood and hopelessness. If you have any of these, raise them with a clinician. (Source: *Insomnia* condition summary.)\n\nThis is general information, not an assessment of your own sleep. A clinician can judge whether your pattern meets the criteria.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
